pan> is mentioned in <span style=\"color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0.2%</span> of papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. </p>Among 2020-2024 papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"office\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">30.4%</span> of reported significant p-values (p < .05) are fragile (.01 \u2264 p < .05) - i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is a much higher rate than the median Psychology paper (median shown in black); relative to other words, papers with <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"office\" </i></span> are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">91<sup>st</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages. Note, strong/weak p-values highly predict replicability.","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"office\" </i></span> tend to be published in higher impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">86<sup>th</sup></span> percentile among all words)  and tend to be cited more (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">83<sup>rd</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are less likely to publish papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"office\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">11<sup>st</sup></span> percentile). "}